    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p25-q3a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Prove that\n$$\\sum_{r=1}^{n}r(r+1)=\\frac13n(n+1)(n+2).$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$.\n\nUse induction and factor out $(k+1)(k+2)$ in the inductive step.",
          "worked": "Base case: for $n=1$, both sides are $2$.\n\nInductive hypothesis: assume $\\sum_{r=1}^{k}r(r+1)=\\frac13k(k+1)(k+2)$ for some $k\\ge1$.\n\nInductive step:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\sum_{r=1}^{k+1}r(r+1)&=\\sum_{r=1}^{k}r(r+1)+(k+1)(k+2)\\\\\\sum_{r=1}^{k+1}r(r+1)&=\\frac13k(k+1)(k+2)+(k+1)(k+2)\\\\\\sum_{r=1}^{k+1}r(r+1)&=(k+1)(k+2)\\left(\\frac{k}{3}+1\\right)\\\\\\sum_{r=1}^{k+1}r(r+1)&=\\frac13(k+1)(k+2)(k+3).\\end{align*}$$\nThus the formula holds for $k+1$. By mathematical induction, it holds for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 80
      },
      {
        "id": "p25-q3b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Prove that\n$$\\sum_{r=1}^{n}r(r+1)(r+2)=\\frac14n(n+1)(n+2)(n+3).$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$.\n\nUse induction and factor out $(k+1)(k+2)(k+3)$.",
          "worked": "Base case: for $n=1$, both sides are $6$.\n\nInductive hypothesis: assume $\\sum_{r=1}^{k}r(r+1)(r+2)=\\frac14k(k+1)(k+2)(k+3)$ for some $k\\ge1$.\n\nInductive step:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\sum_{r=1}^{k+1}r(r+1)(r+2)&=\\sum_{r=1}^{k}r(r+1)(r+2)+(k+1)(k+2)(k+3)\\\\\\sum_{r=1}^{k+1}r(r+1)(r+2)&=\\frac14k(k+1)(k+2)(k+3)+(k+1)(k+2)(k+3)\\\\\\sum_{r=1}^{k+1}r(r+1)(r+2)&=(k+1)(k+2)(k+3)\\left(\\frac{k}{4}+1\\right)\\\\\\sum_{r=1}^{k+1}r(r+1)(r+2)&=\\frac14(k+1)(k+2)(k+3)(k+4).\\end{align*}$$\nThus the formula holds for $k+1$. By mathematical induction, it holds for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 85
      },
      {
        "id": "p25-q3c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Parts (a) and (b) suggest a general pattern, which can also be proved by induction. For each positive integer $p$, show by induction on $n$ that\n$$\\sum_{r=1}^{n}r(r+1)(r+2)\\cdots(r+p-1)=\\frac1{p+1}n(n+1)(n+2)\\cdots(n+p).$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True for all positive integers $p$ and $n$.\n\nFix $p$, check $n=1$, then factor out $(k+1)(k+2)\\cdots(k+p)$ in the inductive step.",
          "worked": "Fix a positive integer $p$.\n\nBase case: for $n=1$, the left-hand side is $1\\cdot2\\cdots p=p!$ and the right-hand side is $\\frac{1\\cdot2\\cdots(p+1)}{p+1}=p!$.\n\nWrite $S_n=\\sum_{r=1}^{n}r(r+1)\\cdots(r+p-1)$. Assume $S_k=\\frac{k(k+1)\\cdots(k+p)}{p+1}$ for some $k\\ge1$.\n\nInductive step:\n$$\\begin{align*}S_{k+1}&=S_k+(k+1)(k+2)\\cdots(k+p)\\\\S_{k+1}&=\\frac{k(k+1)\\cdots(k+p)}{p+1}+(k+1)(k+2)\\cdots(k+p)\\\\S_{k+1}&=(k+1)(k+2)\\cdots(k+p)\\left(\\frac{k}{p+1}+1\\right)\\\\S_{k+1}&=\\frac{(k+1)(k+2)\\cdots(k+p)(k+p+1)}{p+1}.\\end{align*}$$\nThus the formula holds for $k+1$. By mathematical induction, it holds for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$, for each positive integer $p$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 100
      }
    ]
  },
